package <%= basePackage %>.<%= module %>.userside.service;

/**
 * The <%= module %> module's in-process API: the driving adapter a
 * <strong>peer module</strong> consumes.
 *
 * <h2>Why an added context publishes one at all</h2>
 *
 * <p>The context {@code --with-peer-context} scaffolds has no seam —
 * it is a pure consumer, and nothing in the emitted project consumes
 * <em>it</em>. A context you add with {@code keel add module} is
 * different: the next thing you are likely to do is add another one
 * that reaches this one, and a context with no seam can never be
 * reached. So this interface exists from the start, and
 * {@code keel add module <other> --consumes <%= module %>} works
 * without you having to build the seam by hand first.
 *
 * <h2>It is spelled like the skeleton's seam on purpose</h2>
 *
 * <p>{@code greeting} publishes {@code GreetingService.greetingFor},
 * and this is the same shape with this context's name in it. That is
 * not decoration: it means a gateway generated by {@code --consumes}
 * reads identically whether the context it reaches is keel's own
 * skeleton or one you added, and keel needs no table of per-context
 * seam spellings to emit it.
 *
 * <h2>The rule this interface exists to hold</h2>
 *
 * <p>A peer never depends on this module's domain. It declares a
 * driven port in its own vocabulary, implements that port in its own
 * {@code infra/} over this interface, and the assembly binds the two.
 * The build holds the wall: this module declares its own contract
 * non-transitively, so {@code <%= Module %>Command} is not on a
 * consumer's compile classpath and an import of it does not compile.
 */
public interface <%= Module %>Service {

    /** What this module would answer for {@code subject}. */
    String <%= module %>For(String subject);
}
